---
/**
 * What this site is, for a reader who did not build it.
 *
 * **Rewritten by TK-31, because every substantive claim it made was false.**
 * The previous version told a reader that "every page here is a note that was
 * reviewed and explicitly approved for publication, one item at a time" and
 * that a note "appears here only because it was named on an explicit approval
 * list". That described one owner's exporter. This tool publishes a directory
 * of Markdown by default and withholds what the user's `publish.config.yaml`
 * excludes — the opposite arrangement — so the sentence a reader consults *to
 * find out whether the boundary is trustworthy* was the sentence that lied
 * about which direction it runs.
 *
 * **A shipped page rather than a Markdown note the user seeds.** The plan
 * (§4.4) proposes deleting both prose pages and having `init` write them into
 * the notes repository. That is the better end state and it is not reachable
 * from here: `init` does not exist yet — it is TK-32's — and deleting the pages
 * now would remove `/about/` and `/privacy/` from `SITE_MAP` and from three
 * gates, leaving a build with no privacy statement at all in the interval. A
 * page that is true is worth more than a page that is absent, and the sentences
 * below are true of *every* build this tool produces rather than of one corpus.
 *
 * **So it says only what the tool guarantees.** Every claim here is a property
 * of the generator — static output, no analytics, no third-party requests,
 * exclusion by configuration — and none of it describes the user's own
 * editorial process, which this repository cannot know and must not narrate on
 * their behalf. Where the honest answer depends on the operator, it says so and
 * points at them rather than inventing a policy.
 *
 * The prose is in the navigation language and is not translated, which is the
 * same treatment `tests/translations.test.ts` records for this file: it is a
 * document rather than chrome, and translating a document is a content decision
 * this ticket does not own.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, TITLE_SUFFIX } from '../lib/site.ts';
---

<Layout
  title={`About${TITLE_SUFFIX}`}
  description="What this site is, and how its pages came to be here."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      {SITE_NAME} is a reading site. Each page is a note written in Markdown and published as a
      static page, generated ahead of time rather than assembled when you ask for it.
    </p>

    <h2>How pages get here</h2>

    <p>
      The site is built from a set of notes written in Markdown. Publication is the default: a note
      becomes a page unless it was explicitly excluded, and the person who runs the build chooses
      what to exclude by listing patterns in a configuration file, or by marking an individual note.
      An excluded note produces no page, no search entry, and no link, and none of its content
      appears here.
    </p>

    <p>
      Links between notes are resolved when the site is built. A link whose target is not published
      is not rendered as a link, so following one cannot lead to something that is not here.
    </p>

    <p>
      One thing this site can still show you about an unpublished note is the words a published note
      used to refer to it. A link left pointing at an excluded note keeps its own link text, which
      is the author's writing rather than anything read from the note it names. Nothing else about
      unpublished notes — their content, their number, or that any exist — is represented here.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is built</h2>

    <p>
      Every page is generated ahead of time and served as a static file. There is no server-side
      application, no database behind a page view, and no account system. Reading, navigating,
      following links, and browsing backlinks all work with JavaScript turned off.
    </p>

    <p>
      For what this site does and does not collect, see <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
